{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/kernel/kernel.go at exact ea02145f645ec64bc91f9b63333d17fb8e2eba5b, two commits over base d1ae6ade (5977834 the code and test checkpoint, ea02145f the documents) on request/oversized-attachment-disposition (pushed); git merge-tree onto current main 1a5f1586 is clean, tree 6d67d65e. THE REPORTING ARTIFACT for this lane, under promise c2c18651 on request 59154b87 (#22093, the remaining oversized-attachment outcome of #137), delivering the narrowing of decision fcb32a61 (#5075) that Hugh adopted in proposal ddaf3197 (#22135, ratify ecc3db6f), and carrying the correction required by codex's changes-requested verdict b48122a2cf9bb2cddbcf4d43b38424eb29474c19 (ratified 48bbd732) and planner assert c31754cf1152e84076518f42867e7594f58e2f4a. It is the newest artifact on that promise and no separate ready-for-review report is filed. This head replaces the withdrawn e66a2977 on the same live commitment: the outcome, conditions, performer, destination and governing authority are unchanged, so no child request was filed.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE THE REVIEWED HEAD e66a2977. Four files differ, and only one of them is code. The finding, stated the same way by both reviewers: internal/app/app.go skipped kernel.ValidateUnsignedRequestSize when config.PayloadCeiling was zero, so the two supported workrooms that record no local ceiling, a configuration written before the field existed and a read-only attachment, still signed an oversized act. Codex reproduced it by cloning a synthetic workspace's stored configuration with the ceiling set to zero, reopening it and refiling the existing test's 4 KiB act, which produced the correct kernel refusal but one signature; and independently through real AttachConfig, where BuildActRequest returned a verifiable signature and source.AcceptSubmission refused afterwards. The planner's assert reported the same counterexample, measuring 4,802 bytes against a 2,048 byte ceiling with one signature recorded by this head's own signatureCounter. Both asked for the authoritative ceiling to be obtained before signing, and both explicitly forbade solving it by refusing every such workroom outright. The fix removes the skip: Workspace.payloadCeiling returns the local record's ceiling when it has one and otherwise reads the genesis descriptor through kernel.Descriptor, remembering it in an atomic.Uint64 because genesis is immutable, and signRequest measures against that before it signs. The two new controls in internal/app/sign_ceiling_test.go are the legacy case (stored payload_ceiling deleted and the workspace reopened through the ordinary path, so the immutable-config consistency guard cannot be what answers) and the attached case (a fetched refs/seq, AttachConfig recording zero, BuildActRequest driven with the source key). Each refuses the oversized act naming the genesis ceiling of 2048 with zero signatures and no append, then signs an ordinary act exactly once and appends it. architecture.md and limits.md were corrected to say what the code now does, and the app.go behaviour basis in their front matter moved to the successor f51aec8a. The kernel, intent, cmd and service files are byte for byte unchanged from the reviewed head.\n\nWHAT CHANGED AT THIS PATH, internal/kernel/kernel.go, which is unchanged since the reviewed head. The ceiling and its enforcement do not move. validateRequestSize is replaced by one measurement and one judgement: measureRequest walks the envelope, the payload and every attachment into a requestSize (envelope, payload, attachment total, count, largest name and size, and a saturated flag), and requestSize.judge spends the ceiling as a remaining budget, envelope then payload then attachments, which is the accounting this kernel has always applied and cannot overflow. addSize saturates instead of wrapping and says whether it had to; a measurement that saturated is refused outright, because its true total is larger than any ceiling a genesis can record. Submit now calls measureRequest on the envelope it has already built, so the envelope is not built twice. requestSize.refusal replaces the bare string with a diagnostic that opens with the same words ('event exceeds genesis ceiling') so every existing caller and document matching that prefix still matches, then adds the measured total, the ceiling, the split as 'envelope N, payload N, attachments N in N files, largest \"name\" at N bytes' (or 'no attachments'), and the remedy: shrink the evidence, split it across several events, or cite a repository path instead of attaching bytes. The largest attachment is settled by name on a size tie, because Go randomises map order and one request must produce one message. boundedName truncates an attachment name to 128 bytes, since the kernel measures a request before the payload tree writer validates names and a raw submission body can carry a key of any length. Exported ValidateUnsignedRequestSize applies that same measurement to an act that is not signed yet: it checks the actor key length, builds a placeholder request whose signature is 64 zero bytes, and runs measureRequest.judge. Because the actor key and the signature are fixed widths and the intent bytes and causal references are already final, that placeholder measures the byte for byte identical envelope the signed request will carry. ValidateRequestSize, the reader re-checks and the genesis descriptor are untouched.\n\nTHE ADOPTED CONDITIONS OF ddaf3197, ONE BY ONE. (1) The hard 1 MiB genesis ceiling and its enforcement are kept exactly: the same requests are refused as before, pinned by TestSummedAccountingRefusesExactlyWhatTheStepwiseAccountingRefused, which keeps the replaced stepwise accounting verbatim as an oracle. (2) The bare refusal is replaced by an actionable diagnostic naming the ceiling, the measured total, the envelope, payload and attachment split, the largest attachment by name and the remedy, pinned at every boundary. (3) Refusal before signing is delivered at the one place that signs, internal/app signRequest, through the kernel's one measurement of a placeholder signed envelope, measured against the ceiling genesis records, and observed on each of the three previously unchecked surfaces by counting what reaches the transport or the submission path, and now on both zero-local-ceiling workrooms as well. (4) The per-surface preflights that the earlier candidate had added to cmd/gs/main.go, cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go and internal/service/ui.go are removed: all three files are byte for byte identical to d1ae6ade at this head, because one boundary at the signing site covers every surface and three copies of it would be three places to drift. (5) The documents are corrected: the design note's reference-and-locator claim, the gs verify sentence that said only payload sizes are bounded, and limits.md. (6) The census in the packet is replaced by the planner's independent numbers in assert ed87935e (0a048f5d), attributed to it, and the zero-refusal claim is withdrawn. Retry identity is untouched throughout because nothing re-signs and a refused act mints no signature; each surface test refiles under the same idempotency key and lands.\n\nTHE PLANNER'S #22166 FINDINGS, ANSWERED. (1) Before signing. The finding was correct and the proposal named the wrong API: kernel.ValidateRequestSize verifies an already signed request, so a preflight calling it after BuildActRequest refuses before append, not before signing. The condition is met as adopted and without a second size formula, by exporting the kernel's own measurement over a placeholder-signed envelope and applying it inside signRequest before intent.Sign; and, after the verdict and the planner assert above, on every supported workroom rather than only those whose local record mirrors the ceiling. The equality is proved rather than asserted, on the verdict and on the refusal text, at the exact ceiling and one byte under, in TestUnsignedMeasurementEqualsTheSignedMeasurement; a mutant that shortens the placeholder signature to zero length fails it. The configured ceiling is read from the workroom or from genesis, never hard coded. (2) Refused attempts. The zero-refusal claim is withdrawn. limits.md now says that refused admission creates no event, that accepted history therefore cannot show how many attempts were refused or that any were, that no refusal evidence was found in the retained logs that were searched and that the search was limited, and that nobody asking for a larger attachment would appear there either. (3) and (4) Census and the historical comparison. The packet's own script is not published. limits.md carries the planner's independent census, attributed to assert ed87935e and dated 2026-09-09: every one of the 27,845 non-genesis events at four pinned heads, records with and without attachments alike, identical on a second run; 555 events carrying 1,772 attachments; nearest-rank p50 1,279, p90 9,145, p99 60,232, maximum 498,733; largest complete event per room Gitseq 196,373 at 5.34 times headroom, Tailapp 535,397 at 1.96, Chess 86,436 at 12.13, Inventory 31,972 at 32.80, against a recorded ceiling of 1,048,576 in all four; largest attachment-free record 74,031. The five-week headroom-loss claim is deleted, and the page says to keep the rooms distinct because the fleet maximum is Tailapp's and is not Gitseq's headroom. (5) Provenance. The three reference pages carry the commit-1 behaviour artifacts in their front matter (limits.md on kernel e7bd0c2e and app f51aec8a, verify.md on kernel e7bd0c2e, architecture.md on all three including intent cc1d53e5), which is why this head is two commits: the behaviour is checkpointed and published first at 5977834, and the documents cite it. The app basis moved from 865529c7 to its successor f51aec8a because app.go changed in the correction; kernel.go and intent.go are byte for byte identical between 84289545 and 5977834, verified with git diff --stat, so their bases stay. One further finding came out of the pre-publication review rather than from #22166 and is answered in the same head: an untrusted attachment name must not reach an unbounded error string.\n\nOMISSION CONTROLS (evidence/mutants.txt). The thirteen earlier guards were re-run against the delivered source and are all red: kernel diagnostic reverted to the bare string; largest-attachment tie-break removed; untrusted attachment name no longer bounded; saturating attachment total no longer refused; placeholder signature shortened to zero length; actor key length no longer checked; signing check moved behind the signature; signing check removed, observed separately at the application boundary, at gs state, at MCP state and at the resident /v0/act; the zero-ceiling skip restored; and the signature measurement never recorded. Three runs then pin the correction itself. Restoring the zero-ceiling guard, which is the defect exactly as filed, fails the legacy control with 'the refused act was signed 1 time(s)' and fails the attached control with 'an attached workspace signed an act over the genesis ceiling'. The same mutation passes the ordinary-ceiling control, and that pass is recorded deliberately: it is why the two new controls had to be written, because the defect was invisible to the test that already existed. Replacing payloadCeiling's body with the local field, which is the forbidden blanket zero-ceiling refusal rather than the original skip, fails both new controls, each on the refusal naming 'a ceiling of 0' instead of 2048. Two mutants survived earlier in this work and both were fixed rather than argued away, and both are still recorded in the file: the saturation clause survived because the remaining-budget arithmetic already refused every case the oracle table then held, so the table gained a saturating total against a wholly unspent maximum ceiling; and moving the check behind the signature survived because the measurement was recorded beside the signing call rather than inside it, so the record was skipped along with the check, which is why the record moved inside Workspace.sign.\n\nGATES (evidence/gates-v2.txt). Every gate ran at the final code tree, head d2e2fea5, whose code is identical to commit 1 5977834: gofmt -l ./internal ./cmd, go vet ./..., go build ./..., git diff --check, go test -count=1 over kernel, intent, app, service, cmd/gs and cmd/gitseq-mcp, go test -race -count=1 over kernel, app and service, and go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/..., all rc 0. One whole-module go test ./... came back rc 1, and it is recorded rather than hidden: cmd/gs failed at 601 seconds on TestFilingDisclosesItsBasesBeforeSigning and TestBatchProcessReadsItsFileAndReportsFailures, both from the same cause, the one-second 'gs: verifying the durable log; this may take a while' banner that loadSnapshotWithProgress writes to stderr at cmd/gs/main.go:2475. The first test asserts a subprocess's stderr is empty; the second merges stderr into stdout with CombinedOutput and parses it as JSON. cmd/gs/main.go is byte for byte identical to d1ae6ade in this head and nothing here touches the snapshot path; codex has since fixed the batch-test half of this on main, after this branch's base. It was reproduced directly under load, failing once in three attempts at 33.0 seconds against 20.2 and 20.9 for the passes, with six further runs after the load dropped all passing and an unloaded run at 9.8 seconds. The clean repeat, go test -count=1 -timeout 30m ./... (full output in wholemodule-rerun.txt), passed every package with cmd/gs at 519.6 seconds, inside the ordinary 600 second timeout, so the longer timeout was precaution and not what made it pass. Commit 2 was then re-amended to move the app.go basis to f51aec8a, and gofmt, go vet, go build, git diff --check and ./internal/docset/... were re-run at ea02145f, all rc 0. State honestly: one whole-module run was red on two load-sensitive tests in an untouched file, and the repeat was green.\n\nWHAT IS NOT DONE, AND WHY. The locator path is not implemented: the adopted outcome withdrew it. The genesis ceiling is not raised, which would need a new workroom and is excluded by the request. Reading attachments back through gs inspect is a separate reading-surface outcome and is untouched. The resident's 2 MiB body cap is recorded in limits.md but not changed, and the deliberate asymmetry between batch and merge planning, which measure it themselves, and a single act, which learns from the resident one round trip later, is documented rather than removed. The assessment document itself is not published as a repository file; its corrected figures live in limits.md attributed to the planner's census. The load-sensitive cmd/gs stderr tests are not repaired here: cmd/gs/main.go is untouched by this head and that repair landed separately on main. No genesis change, migration, external storage, automatic URL access, key operation, resident restart or deployment is part of this head. No UI source changes and the embed is unchanged. The artifact at this path at the withdrawn head e66a2977 (4243728e) is outside the merge target and is not a predecessor here; its author retires it separately. Succeeds the live artifacts at this path.","body":{"branch":"request/oversized-attachment-disposition","commit":"ea02145f645ec64bc91f9b63333d17fb8e2eba5b","path":"internal/kernel/kernel.go","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c2c186516fa6f2df92a9b2d23f605c5bf744c388 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015bb3acec403ed0d43bcd2a3c38bd188f7740ef, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02bae1e811fde13104ef614631ac608dca7a7295 and 171 more"}}